        "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence prior = 20.45 million claims from 2018 IRS Table 3.3-derived reference, historical sample = 2016 ACTC 18.921, 2018 refundable CTC/ACTC 20.45, 2021 full-refundable 37.77, and 2021 advance-payment 36.049 million. A formal time-series model is not used because the usable regime-consistent sample is sparse and structurally broken by ARPA. Adjustment components: +0.4 million demographic/filing drift from 2018 to TY2027, +1.1 million for families with earnings from $1 to $2,500 who gain small refundable amounts, -0.6 million because some newly eligible units have too little benefit, missing SSNs, no return filing, or no qualifying child under the 2027 rules. Point = 20.45 + 0.4 + 1.1 - 0.6 = 21.35, rounded to 21.4 million. For interval sizing, use stable-regime changes rather than the one-year ARPA regime jump: 2016 to 2018 change = 20.45 - 18.921 = 1.529 million; explicit judgmental policy-mechanism uncertainty add-on = 0.65 million, so sigma = sqrt(1.529^2 + 0.65^2) = 1.66 million. 1.28*sigma = 2.13 million; I widen to 2.8 million because the target is conditional on a new law and IRS first-print take-up can deviate, giving 21.4 +/- 2.8 = 18.6 to 24.2 million."
